          "page": 1,
          "quote": "Redis AOF persistence records every write command.",
          "section": "",
          "source": "sample.pdf"
        }
      ],
      "conclusion": "The imported documents support only a minimal description of Redis AOF persistence: AOF records every write command and persists writes to an append-only file, and it is contrasted with RDB, which creates periodic snapshots for backups. Two independent sources agree on the append-only-file/write-recording characterization. No retrieved evidence covers fsync policies (always/everysec/no), AOF rewrite or background rewrite buffers, file format details, recovery behavior, or any performance measurement, so production throughput cannot be confirmed from these documents.",
      "disagreements": [],
      "findings": [
        {
          "citation_ids": [
            "E-e1bd425d-ef08-5201-bd0b-e6de14504954"
          ],
          "text": "AOF persistence is described as recording every write command, and is distinguished from RDB, which creates periodic snapshots for backups. This is the only source that mentions RDB as a contrast."
        },
        {
          "citation_ids": [
            "E-d00ec1e6-0304-5b00-89a6-272d0798ab8b"
          ],
          "text": "A second, independent document corroborates the core mechanism: Redis AOF persists writes to an append-only file. Together with the sample.pdf statement, this is the only cross-source agreement found."
        },
        {
          "citation_ids": [
            "E-5441ddfe-97d5-5f6b-b4cf-1cde509513e3",
            "E-9044aa59-86ab-5192-8014-28bb12aa0bc5"
          ],
          "text": "The remaining retrieved chunks are unrelated to Redis persistence (a vector index readiness probe and a statement about Qdrant storing dense vectors and metadata); they provide no supporting or contradicting detail on AOF."
        }
      ],
      "insufficient_evidence": false,
      "limitations": [
        "No retrieved evidence describes fsync/appendfsync policies (always, everysec, no) or the resulting durability trade-offs, so durability guarantees cannot be characterized.",
